// =============================================================================
//  probe_session.hml — AN HOUR, MEASURED.  "find-the-hour", W7 diagnostic.
// =============================================================================
//
//      hemlockc -O1 tools/probe_session.hml -o /tmp/probe_session
//      /tmp/probe_session                         # COMPILED — the shipping path
//      nm /tmp/probe_session | grep -c SDL_       # MUST be 0
//
//  Exits 1 on any failed assertion and asserts its own assertion COUNT, so a
//  section that silently stops running is a FAILURE rather than a green run.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Three consecutive gates have said the same eight words about this game:
//  "worth twenty minutes, and that is a real change."  Nobody has established
//  WHY it stops at twenty.  Every wave so far has guessed at what the hour
//  needs by reading the design documents.  This probe measures it instead.
//
//  It runs ONE FULL HOUR of game time — 3600 s, 216 000 ticks, 3.75 of the
//  16-minute day — through the REAL server and the REAL client, and samples
//  thirty-one numbers once per game minute.  The output is a minute-by-minute
//  account of what the player has, and the whole point of it is that the
//  columns STOP MOVING somewhere, and this file says where.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  THE TWO ARMS, AND WHY BOTH ARE NEEDED
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  ARM POST — `src/game/demobot.hml`, THE SHIPPING DRIVER, imported and not
//      ported (the lesson of probe_firstminute: a port is how 10-of-12 and
//      0-of-8 became two truths about one game).  It leashes to the first
//      lantern post at 16 m and never leaves.  This is EXACTLY what
//      `main.hml --demo 3600` on a real display is doing, so the numbers here
//      and the pictures there describe one session.
//      It is the "stand and fight forever" hour.
//
//  ARM WALK — a LAMPLIGHTER.  It does what DIRECTION.md says the game is: it
//      fights the night at the post, and at dawn it walks the ~282 m to the
//      settlement, greets every villager it can reach, buys everything the
//      shop will sell it, and walks back out.  Every in-game day.  This is the
//      arm that answers "does the loop LOOP".
//
//  There is a THIRD instrument inside this file and it is not an arm: the
//  SHADOW SETTLEMENT (see the block above `shadow_new`).  It runs a second,
//  private copy of the SHIPPED `settle.hml` and `npc.hml` alongside the real
//  session, fed from `w.anim` instead of `w.model`, so that "what the one-word
//  fix would do" is a MEASUREMENT rather than a hope.  Plus
//  `what_would_it_take()`, which asks `settle.hml` directly how many lit posts
//  at what radius it wants before it will call the place a Village.
//
//  ARM WALK IS AN ORACLE PLAYER AND THAT IS DECLARED, NOT HIDDEN.  It reads
//  villager positions straight out of `npc_x`/`npc_z` on the server rather
//  than finding them on screen, and it knows the settlement centre from
//  `client_settle_x`.  A human learns both facts in their first five minutes
//  and then has them forever, so the shortcut costs the measurement nothing
//  that matters — but it means this arm measures WHAT THE SYSTEMS DO WHEN
//  DRIVEN CORRECTLY, and NOT how hard they are to discover.  Discoverability
//  is measured with eyes, on a display, and that half of the task is in
//  `docs/design/THE_HOUR.md`, which is where the whole diagnosis lives.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  IMPORTS — this is a tool, so the wall (CLAUDE.md §3) permits src/game/**.
//  It links NO SDL and NO wobbleweed, and `nm | grep -c SDL_` must print 0.
// =============================================================================

